{# Widget "zones" — one row per zone, with the swatch the plate draws it in.

   THE SWATCH IS A CATEGORY POSITION AND NEVER A COLOUR: the zone's place in
   the set picks the house's nth category token, so the same position gives
   the same token here and on the plate. This module names no value. #}
{# THE LIST IS THE WIDGET, AND THE LIST IS THE ROOT.
   Everything this list draws is scoped to `.fg-lst`, never to the rail that
   happens to hold it: the same markup is rendered in the rail beside the
   plate AND at full size in the widget library, and a rule scoped to the
   rail would make the library show raw text. The rail's own chrome — the
   cell, its head, the scroller — stays on `.fg-side`, and the rail passes
   `cell` and `head` to ask for it.

   THE ROW CHANGES THE PLATE IN PLACE. `data-atlas-swap` is the atlas's own
   verb: it fetches where the row already points, takes the plate's subtrees
   out of the answer and puts them in place of the live ones, and brings ONE
   named region across with them — this list, so the row that was clicked
   comes back marked. The server still decides everything; this module
   writes an attribute and no map code at all.

   THE HREF IS UNCHANGED AND STILL DOES THE WHOLE JOB: with no script, on a
   middle click, or if anything goes wrong, the browser navigates and the
   same page arrives the ordinary way.

   ONLY IN THE RAIL. The library renders this same list as a twin — three
   times over, from one render — so a row there must centre nothing and the
   root must carry no id there would then be three of. The rail asks for
   both by naming the list; nobody else does. #}
{# A ROW GOES SOMEWHERE ONLY WHERE THERE IS A PLATE TO MOVE. The row
   keeps its shape either way — same element, same classes, same centre
   mark — because a twin in the library is a picture of this row and must
   measure like one. What it does not keep is the destination: a picture
   that quietly navigated somewhere would be the preview behaving as the
   thing it stands for, and an anchor with no href is inert without
   looking like anything else. #}
{% set centring = listId|default('') %}
<div class="fg-lst{{ cell|default('') }}"{% if listId|default('') %} id="{{ listId }}"{% endif %} data-list="zones">
{% if head|default(false) %}{{ include('@UhifadhiRoster/rail/_cell.html.twig', {id: zones.id, label: zones.label, count: zones.count, position: position, total: total, mayCompose: mayCompose}, with_context: false) }}{% endif %}
{% for section in zones.sections %}
    <div class="grp gsub">{{ section.label }}</div>
    {% for row in section.rows %}
        {% if row.centres %}
            <a class="fg-zn{{ row.quiet ? ' quiet' : '' }}{{ row.centre == centre ? ' on' : '' }}" {% if centring %} href="?centre={{ row.centre }}" data-atlas-swap data-atlas-swap-also="#{{ listId }}"{% endif %}>
        {% else %}
            <div class="fg-zn{{ row.quiet ? ' quiet' : '' }}">
        {% endif %}
            <i class="zsw" style="background:var(--cat-{{ row.category }})"></i>
            <span class="nm">{{ row.name }}<em>{{ row.detail }}</em></span>
            <span class="lv{{ row.live == 0 ? ' none' : '' }}">
                {%- if row.live > 0 %}<i class="livedot"></i>{% endif %}{{ row.live }} live
            </span>
            {% if row.centres %}<span class="loc">{{ ux_icon('roster:locate') }}</span>{% endif %}
        {% if row.centres %}</a>{% else %}</div>{% endif %}
    {% endfor %}
{% endfor %}
</div>
